const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Will a razor blade scratch my window glass?",
    answer: "A new, sharp blade held flat on wet glass and pushed in one direction usually won't scratch ordinary glass. Scratches come from dull or rusty blades, dry glass, dragging the blade backward, and debris trapped under the blade. Tempered glass is the exception — see the next question.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I use a razor blade on tempered glass?",
    answer: "Carefully, if at all. Tempered glass (sliding doors, glass near doors, large low windows, and many newer windows) can have tiny particles fused to its surface during manufacturing. A blade can catch one and drag a long scratch. Test in a corner and lean on solvents first.",
  },
  {
    question: "What's the best way to get latex paint off glass?",
    answer: "Soak the paint with warm soapy water for a few minutes to soften it, then lift it with a fresh blade or plastic scraper while the glass stays wet. Most latex releases cleanly once it's softened.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I remove oil-based paint from windows?",
    answer: "Soften it with mineral spirits on a cloth, give it a few minutes, then scrape gently with a wet blade. Wash the glass afterward to remove the solvent film, and keep solvents off vinyl frames as much as possible.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can paint be removed from window screens or frames?",
    answer: "Fresh latex on vinyl or aluminum frames usually wipes off with soapy water. Dried paint on frames and screens is harder — scraping vinyl damages it — so it's often better to touch up the frame than to scrape aggressively.",
  },
];
