<GuideLayout
  slug="removing-paint-from-window-glass"
  title="How to Remove Dried Paint From Window Glass Without Scratching"
  description="How to get latex and oil-based paint off window glass safely: the wet-blade method, when not to use a razor (tempered glass, film, coatings), and the mistakes that cause scratches."
  heading="How to Get Dried Paint Off Window Glass Without Scratching It"
  lede="Paint specks after a painting job are one of the most common window problems. Removing them is easy — scratching the glass in the process is even easier. Here's how to do it right."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>Exterior painting, interior trim work, and DIY projects all leave paint on glass. The good news: paint sits on top of the glass and almost always comes off completely. The risk is in the removal — most scratched windows come from someone scraping dry glass with an old blade.</p>

  <h2>If the Paint Is Still Wet</h2>

  <p>Wipe it off immediately with a damp cloth. Wet latex comes off glass with no effort at all. Even a few hours later, a soak with soapy water usually lets it wipe away.</p>

  <h2>Removing Dried Paint: The Wet-Blade Method</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Check the glass first.</strong> Is it tempered (often marked with a small etched logo in a corner)? Is there tint or window film? Is it old, wavy historic glass? All three call for extra caution — see below.</li>
    <li><strong>Wash the glass</strong> to remove grit. Grit trapped under a blade is what causes scratches.</li>
    <li><strong>Soften the paint.</strong> Soapy water for latex, mineral spirits for oil-based. Give it a few minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Use a brand-new blade</strong> in a holder, held at a low angle (around 30 degrees or flatter).</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the glass wet</strong> the entire time and push the blade forward only. Never pull it back across the glass.</li>
    <li><strong>Test in a corner first,</strong> then work in short strokes, wiping the blade often.</li>
    <li><strong>Wash and squeegee</strong> the window and check it in direct sunlight.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> If you feel the blade catch or hear grinding, stop. That's debris under the blade, and continuing will scratch the glass.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>When Not to Use a Razor Blade</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tempered glass:</strong> manufacturing debris fused to the surface can catch on a blade and leave long scratches. Use solvent and a plastic scraper first.</li>
    <li><strong>Tinted or filmed windows:</strong> a blade will cut the film.</li>
    <li><strong>Low-E or coated surfaces:</strong> some coatings face the inside of the house; scraping can damage them.</li>
    <li><strong>Historic glass:</strong> older glass in Covington, Newport, and Fort Thomas homes is softer and often already marked. Go slowly and favor solvents.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What About Paint Splatter on Storm Doors and Decorative Glass?</h2>

  <p>Decorative and etched panels are expensive to replace, so they deserve the most patience. Soften the paint longer, use a plastic scraper before trying a blade, and stay off any etched or frosted areas entirely — solvent and a soft cloth are the only safe tools there.</p>

  <h2>When to Call a Pro</h2>

  <p>If paint covers a lot of windows — after an exterior repaint, for example — or it's on tempered, tinted, or decorative glass, it's usually worth having a window cleaner do it. Paint removal is a routine part of <a href="/guides/post-construction-window-cleaning/">post-construction cleaning</a>, and it's typically quoted as an add-on to a normal cleaning.</p>
